# Do not install devDependencies, and read this before removing it.
#
# This service has none of its own, so on the face of it the setting does
# nothing. What it is actually for is the `"ldapjs": "file:node-ldapjs"`
# dependency — the git submodule beside this file. npm installs the
# devDependencies of a local-path dependency along with its runtime ones, and
# ldapjs's are tap, eslint and their trees: roughly 200 extra packages and a
# dozen advisories, none of which this service loads or ships.
#
# Without this line a bare `npm install` here produces an `npm audit` report
# full of findings against a test runner that is not part of the product, which
# is the kind of noise that gets a real finding ignored. The Dockerfile passes
# --omit=dev as well, so losing this file does not change the image; it changes
# what a developer sees on their own checkout.
#
# One of those devDependencies does more than sit there. @fastify/pre-commit
# INSTALLS A GIT HOOK, and npm runs a local-path dependency's install scripts from
# THIS package root, so the script walks up to the first `.git` it finds — this
# repository's, not ldapjs's — and wires ldapjs's lint and tap suite into commits
# here. An install that predates this line has already done it once: the hook
# survives, the package it calls does not, and every `git commit` afterwards fails
# with `./node_modules/@fastify/pre-commit/hook: No such file or directory`. The
# fix is `rm .git/hooks/pre-commit` — this repository has no pre-commit checks of
# its own, so there is nothing in that file worth keeping.
omit=dev
